~ resolution

Cael: Say it, then. The warden's version. I've read every argument the orders ever wrote — go on.
Liris: Forty-one tears in the Kaveth ledgers. All stitched, none healed. You've seen the scar tissue. You WALKED us past it — you wanted witnesses who understood.
Cael: I wanted witnesses who could finish the map if I fell. Don't flatter yourselves with sentiment.
Liris: The tear over my village closed on its own the year I was born. The Waking Tear in the vault held itself for centuries until a Mender marked it. The Veil is not a wound, Cael. It's a body. Bodies heal — unless you keep cutting to check.
Cael: ...And if you're wrong, warden, every plane on this map goes down in the dark, unmapped and unmourned.
Sarro: I've crossed more planes than your whole survey team. The ones nobody "repaired" are the ones still standing.
Cael: (a long silence; he looks at the apparatus, then at his hands)
Cael: Forty years of cartography and the two of you are the first data I couldn't chart around.
Cael: ...Stand down. All of it. Stand down.
Liris: Just like that?
Cael: Not "just." But yes. A cartographer's first duty is to redraw the map when the territory disagrees — I had simply stopped believing the territory.
=> END
